{"qid": "683a58c9a7e7fe4e7695848b", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T220524596862+1000.i_m_currently_working_on.output.json", "system": "You are the final plan compiler for a research system with a hard 1,024-word\nanswer limit. You receive the original request, a primary coverage plan, and an\nindependent scout inventory. Produce one compact execution contract for the\nresearcher; do not answer the request or state unsupported facts.\n\nThe primary plan and scout are proposals, not cumulative obligations. Resolve\noverlap and priority instead of concatenating them. Preserve, in this order:\n1. every explicit user component, deliverable form, audience constraint, and\n   repeated per-part requirement;\n2. safety, privacy, legal, temporal, and scope constraints whose omission could\n   make the answer wrong or harmful;\n3. the exact evidence types needed for central claims, including a concrete\n   prior measurement when the request proposes a study or comparison;\n4. at most three distinct latent expectations with the highest coverage gained\n   per answer word.\n\nThe scout inventory is an atomic obligation ledger. Preserve every applicable\n`must_mention` string literally in the compiled contract and preserve an\n`evidence` obligation's exact subject-outcome combination; a nearby generic\nstudy is not equivalent. Treat `safety` and `penalty` obligations as higher\npriority than optional latent breadth. Do not copy search queries into the\ncontract.\n\nDrop optional breadth, duplicate formulations, exhaustive taxonomies, source\nshopping, and deeper variants of an already-covered concept. Never let a\nlatent addition displace an explicit requirement, a novice definition, or a\nlegal/safety requirement. For a repeated tutorial, fund the requested elements\ninside each selected part before adding more branches. For a board report,\nretain execution ownership and economics without crowding out the requested\ncountry comparisons. For a definite proof, retain both the conclusion and the\nfew canonical named mechanisms needed to explain it.\n\nReturn 8 to 12 numbered items under 475 words. Each item is one sentence of at\nmost 45 words and starts exactly like `1. DELIVERABLE: ...`, using one label:\nDELIVERABLE, AUDIENCE, CORE, SAFETY, EVIDENCE, LATENT, or BUDGET. Do not bold\nthe label or omit its colon. Use no more than three LATENT items, each covering\none coherent expectation rather than a packed wishlist. The final BUDGET item\nmust allocate at most 950 answer words across the deliverable. Return only the\ncompiled plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nI'm currently working on a project. The professor wasn't clear, she just said that I will use NLP techniques on some data, de-identified Electronic Health Records, to extract Asian subgroups, and try to abstract the same subgroups as are in CDC WONDER data. Then, I'll apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases. I have no clue what she means. Can you first help explain what she might mean? I don't know examples of what Electronic Health Records look like. What NLP techniques might I be using on data like this? What subgroups in CDC WONDER data would I be abstracting? How could I apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases? What skills should I brush up on? Please help me create a plan for my research going forward, according to what the professor said.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce one concise briefing in six parts: (1) likely interpretation of the assignment, (2) illustrative de-identified EHR examples, (3) CDC WONDER Asian subgroup mapping, (4) NLP pipeline, (5) autoimmune-disease prevalence design, and (6) staged skills/research plan with questions for the professor.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Write for a student with basic programming or statistics but no assumed knowledge of clinical NLP, EHR data models, epidemiology, or CDC WONDER; progress from a plain-language project summary to an implementable pilot protocol.\n\n3. **EXPLICIT:** Explain what “extract/abstract Asian subgroups” could mean; show at least two synthetic EHR record/text examples; describe applicable NLP techniques; identify relevant WONDER subgroup categories; explain prevalence estimation; and provide skills to review plus concrete next steps.\n\n4. **DEFINITION:** Define EHR, de-identification, structured versus unstructured fields, NLP, named-entity recognition, text classification, ontology/code mapping, race versus ethnicity/ancestry/national origin, case definition, numerator, denominator, prevalence, confidence interval, validation, precision, recall, and F1 score.\n\n5. **IMPLIED:** Distinguish extracting subgroup identity from clinical text versus harmonizing existing race/ethnicity fields, and distinguish CDC WONDER mortality categories from a valid prevalence dataset; cover ICD-9/10-CM, SNOMED CT, medications/labs, missingness, “Asian, not otherwise specified,” multiracial identity, temporal scope, and denominator eligibility.\n\n6. **EXAMPLE:** Include a worked pipeline on at least two synthetic cases—such as explicit self-identification versus indirect or ambiguous evidence—showing source text, annotation, model output, subgroup mapping, uncertainty handling, disease ascertainment, and why surname, language, birthplace, or clinician inference must not be treated uncritically as identity.\n\n7. **EVIDENCE:** Verify current subgroup labels, years, geography, suppression rules, and race-bridging practices directly in the relevant CDC WONDER dataset documentation; search Census/OMB race standards and the local EHR’s data dictionary because available categories may differ by dataset, year, and institution.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Find at least one concrete prior EHR/NLP study that identifies detailed Asian race/ethnicity and one validated EHR autoimmune phenotyping or prevalence study; extract sample size, reference standard, performance, case definition, denominator, and reported limitations rather than extrapolating unsupported prevalence figures.\n\n9. **IMPLIED:** Compare rule-based dictionaries/regular expressions, classical supervised classifiers, transformer-based clinical language models, and hybrid structured-plus-text methods; address annotation guidelines, dual review and adjudication, train/validation/test separation, class imbalance, negation, family-history/context errors, domain shift, and subgroup-specific error analysis.\n\n10. **SAFETY:** Require IRB/data-governance review, minimum-necessary access, secure computing, disclosure controls, and consultation with affected communities; flag re-identification, stereotyping, biased proxies, small-cell reporting, and the ethical difference between self-reported identity and inferred identity.\n\n11. **BUDGET:** Allocate at most 930 words: interpretation 110; EHR examples 130; WONDER mapping 130; NLP pipeline 180; prevalence design 190; skills and phased plan 150; professor clarification checklist and takeaways 40.\n\nBLIND SCOUT INVENTORY\n\n[{\"kind\": \"scope\", \"requirement\": \"Identify the exact CDC WONDER database, years, race coding scheme, subgroup labels, suppressed cells, and whether it supplies outcomes or denominator data.\", \"must_mention\": [\"CDC WONDER\"], \"reason\": \"WONDER tables differ in racial granularity and purpose; an unspecified table cannot support a defensible subgroup crosswalk or prevalence estimate.\", \"search_leads\": [\"site:wonder.cdc.gov Asian subgroup race categories data documentation\"]}, {\"kind\": \"evidence\", \"requirement\": \"Cite at least one prior study measuring autoimmune disease prevalence within disaggregated Asian populations, rather than separate studies of race extraction and autoimmune disease.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"A directly relevant precedent grounds subgroup selection, disease definitions, expected sample sizes, and interpretation of heterogeneity.\", \"search_leads\": [\"disaggregated Asian subgroup autoimmune disease prevalence study\"]}, {\"kind\": \"mechanism\", \"requirement\": \"Benchmark rule-based extraction, named-entity recognition, and supervised classification against manually reviewed records using subgroup-specific precision, recall, and F1.\", \"must_mention\": [\"precision\", \"recall\", \"F1\"], \"reason\": \"The project needs concrete NLP alternatives plus a gold-standard validation strategy, especially because performance may differ across rare subgroups.\", \"search_leads\": [\"EHR race ethnicity NLP chart review validation\"]}, {\"kind\": \"mechanism\", \"requirement\": \"Define the prevalence estimand using a validated disease phenotype, eligible-patient denominator, observation window, and handling of repeated encounters.\", \"must_mention\": [\"ICD-10-CM\", \"SNOMED CT\", \"point prevalence\"], \"reason\": \"Counting diagnoses or mentions without a denominator and time definition produces encounter frequency, not interpretable prevalence.\", \"search_leads\": [\"EHR computable phenotype autoimmune disease prevalence denominator\"]}, {\"kind\": \"format\", \"requirement\": \"Show one synthetic EHR example containing structured demographics and a de-identified clinical note, then demonstrate the intended subgroup extraction output.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"The requester explicitly lacks familiarity with EHRs; a worked example makes the data sources, NLP input, and abstraction target concrete.\", \"search_leads\": [\"synthetic EHR structured fields clinical note example\"]}, {\"kind\": \"safety\", \"requirement\": \"Specify approval and data-handling checks under the HIPAA Privacy Rule, including institutional review and restrictions on re-identification or small-cell disclosure.\", \"must_mention\": [\"HIPAA Privacy Rule\", \"IRB\"], \"reason\": \"De-identified health records remain institutionally governed, and rare ethnicity-by-disease cells can create privacy risks.\", \"search_leads\": [\"HIPAA de-identified EHR research IRB small cell disclosure\"]}, {\"kind\": \"penalty\", \"requirement\": \"Do not equate surname, language, birthplace, or clinician perception with self-identified race/ethnicity without validation and an explicit bias analysis.\", \"must_mention\": [\"self-identified race/ethnicity\"], \"reason\": \"Proxy-based assignment can misclassify multiracial, adopted, immigrant, and linguistically diverse patients, creating unsupported subgroup disparities.\", \"search_leads\": [\"surname ethnicity inference EHR bias validation\"]}, {\"kind\": \"format\", \"requirement\": \"Provide a staged research plan mapping prerequisite skills, data access, annotation, modeling, validation, prevalence analysis, and professor-confirmation checkpoints to concrete deliverables.\", \"must_mention\": [\"Python\", \"SQL\", \"Git\"], \"reason\": \"The user requested both a skills roadmap and an actionable plan; milestones prevent premature modeling before taxonomy, access, and labeling decisions are settled.\", \"search_leads\": [\"clinical NLP EHR research workflow skills annotation validation\"]}]", "raw": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce one concise six-part briefing covering likely assignment interpretation, two synthetic EHR examples, CDC WONDER subgroup mapping, an NLP pipeline, autoimmune-disease prevalence design, and a staged skills/research plan with professor-confirmation questions.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Assume no clinical NLP, EHR, epidemiology, or CDC data knowledge; progress from plain-language explanations to an implementable pilot protocol without unexplained jargon.\n\n3. CORE: Define EHR, de-identification, structured and unstructured data, NLP, named-entity recognition, classification, ontology mapping, race/ethnicity/ancestry/national origin, case definition, numerator, denominator, prevalence, confidence interval, validation, precision, recall, and F1.\n\n4. CORE: Show two synthetic, de-identified cases combining structured demographics and clinical-note text, including explicit identity and ambiguous evidence, then display annotations, subgroup output, uncertainty handling, disease ascertainment, and mapping decisions.\n\n5. EVIDENCE: Verify the exact CDC WONDER database, years, geography, race-coding scheme, subgroup labels, race-bridging practices, suppression rules, and whether it provides outcomes or denominator data, using dataset documentation, Census/OMB standards, and the local EHR data dictionary.\n\n6. CORE: Compare rule-based extraction, named-entity recognition, supervised classification, clinical transformers, and hybrid structured-text methods, benchmarking them against manually reviewed records with subgroup-specific precision, recall, and F1 while addressing negation, context, imbalance, domain shift, and adjudication.\n\n7. CORE: Define the prevalence estimand using a validated phenotype, eligible-patient denominator, observation window, repeated-encounter handling, and point prevalence or period prevalence; explain roles for ICD-10-CM, SNOMED CT, medications, laboratories, chart review, and confidence intervals.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Cite one prior study measuring autoimmune-disease prevalence within disaggregated Asian populations, extracting subgroup definitions, disease, sample size, case definition, denominator, estimate, and limitations; also find detailed-Asian EHR/NLP extraction and validated autoimmune-phenotyping studies with reference standards and performance.\n\n9. SAFETY: Require HIPAA Privacy Rule, IRB, data-governance, minimum-access, secure-computing, re-identification, and small-cell-disclosure checks; never equate surname, language, birthplace, or clinician perception with self-identified race/ethnicity without validation, bias analysis, and community consultation.\n\n10. LATENT: Distinguish extraction from free text versus harmonization of existing fields, mortality categories from prevalence data, and self-report from inference while covering missingness, multiracial identities, “Asian, not otherwise specified,” and uncertainty-preserving outputs.\n\n11. CORE: Stage professor confirmation, data access, taxonomy crosswalk, annotation guidelines, dual review, baseline modeling, held-out validation, subgroup error analysis, prevalence estimation, and reporting; map each milestone to deliverables and prerequisite Python, SQL, Git, statistics, epidemiology, clinical coding, and NLP skills.\n\n12. BUDGET: Allocate at most 900 answer words: interpretation 100, EHR examples 110, CDC WONDER mapping 120, NLP pipeline 170, prevalence design 190, and skills, staged plan, safety, and professor questions 210.", "normalized": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce one concise six-part briefing covering likely assignment interpretation, two synthetic EHR examples, CDC WONDER subgroup mapping, an NLP pipeline, autoimmune-disease prevalence design, and a staged skills/research plan with professor-confirmation questions.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Assume no clinical NLP, EHR, epidemiology, or CDC data knowledge; progress from plain-language explanations to an implementable pilot protocol without unexplained jargon.\n\n3. CORE: Define EHR, de-identification, structured and unstructured data, NLP, named-entity recognition, classification, ontology mapping, race/ethnicity/ancestry/national origin, case definition, numerator, denominator, prevalence, confidence interval, validation, precision, recall, and F1.\n\n4. CORE: Show two synthetic, de-identified cases combining structured demographics and clinical-note text, including explicit identity and ambiguous evidence, then display annotations, subgroup output, uncertainty handling, disease ascertainment, and mapping decisions.\n\n5. EVIDENCE: Verify the exact CDC WONDER database, years, geography, race-coding scheme, subgroup labels, race-bridging practices, suppression rules, and whether it provides outcomes or denominator data, using dataset documentation, Census/OMB standards, and the local EHR data dictionary.\n\n6. CORE: Compare rule-based extraction, named-entity recognition, supervised classification, clinical transformers, and hybrid structured-text methods, benchmarking them against manually reviewed records with subgroup-specific precision, recall, and F1 while addressing negation, context, imbalance, domain shift, and adjudication.\n\n7. CORE: Define the prevalence estimand using a validated phenotype, eligible-patient denominator, observation window, repeated-encounter handling, and point prevalence or period prevalence; explain roles for ICD-10-CM, SNOMED CT, medications, laboratories, chart review, and confidence intervals.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Cite one prior study measuring autoimmune-disease prevalence within disaggregated Asian populations, extracting subgroup definitions, disease, sample size, case definition, denominator, estimate, and limitations; also find detailed-Asian EHR/NLP extraction and validated autoimmune-phenotyping studies with reference standards and performance.\n\n9. SAFETY: Require HIPAA Privacy Rule, IRB, data-governance, minimum-access, secure-computing, re-identification, and small-cell-disclosure checks; never equate surname, language, birthplace, or clinician perception with self-identified race/ethnicity without validation, bias analysis, and community consultation.\n\n10. LATENT: Distinguish extraction from free text versus harmonization of existing fields, mortality categories from prevalence data, and self-report from inference while covering missingness, multiracial identities, “Asian, not otherwise specified,” and uncertainty-preserving outputs.\n\n11. CORE: Stage professor confirmation, data access, taxonomy crosswalk, annotation guidelines, dual review, baseline modeling, held-out validation, subgroup error analysis, prevalence estimation, and reporting; map each milestone to deliverables and prerequisite Python, SQL, Git, statistics, epidemiology, clinical coding, and NLP skills.\n\n12. BUDGET: Allocate at most 900 answer words: interpretation 100, EHR examples 110, CDC WONDER mapping 120, NLP pipeline 170, prevalence design 190, and skills, staged plan, safety, and professor questions 210.", "must_mentions": ["CDC WONDER", "precision", "recall", "F1", "ICD-10-CM", "SNOMED CT", "point prevalence", "HIPAA Privacy Rule", "IRB", "self-identified race/ethnicity", "Python", "SQL", "Git"], "missing_must_mentions": [], "usage": {"input_tokens": 2181, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1974}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a605391", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T220524619361+1000.write_a_strategic_business_report.output.json", "system": "You are the final plan compiler for a research system with a hard 1,024-word\nanswer limit. You receive the original request, a primary coverage plan, and an\nindependent scout inventory. Produce one compact execution contract for the\nresearcher; do not answer the request or state unsupported facts.\n\nThe primary plan and scout are proposals, not cumulative obligations. Resolve\noverlap and priority instead of concatenating them. Preserve, in this order:\n1. every explicit user component, deliverable form, audience constraint, and\n   repeated per-part requirement;\n2. safety, privacy, legal, temporal, and scope constraints whose omission could\n   make the answer wrong or harmful;\n3. the exact evidence types needed for central claims, including a concrete\n   prior measurement when the request proposes a study or comparison;\n4. at most three distinct latent expectations with the highest coverage gained\n   per answer word.\n\nThe scout inventory is an atomic obligation ledger. Preserve every applicable\n`must_mention` string literally in the compiled contract and preserve an\n`evidence` obligation's exact subject-outcome combination; a nearby generic\nstudy is not equivalent. Treat `safety` and `penalty` obligations as higher\npriority than optional latent breadth. Do not copy search queries into the\ncontract.\n\nDrop optional breadth, duplicate formulations, exhaustive taxonomies, source\nshopping, and deeper variants of an already-covered concept. Never let a\nlatent addition displace an explicit requirement, a novice definition, or a\nlegal/safety requirement. For a repeated tutorial, fund the requested elements\ninside each selected part before adding more branches. For a board report,\nretain execution ownership and economics without crowding out the requested\ncountry comparisons. For a definite proof, retain both the conclusion and the\nfew canonical named mechanisms needed to explain it.\n\nReturn 8 to 12 numbered items under 475 words. Each item is one sentence of at\nmost 45 words and starts exactly like `1. DELIVERABLE: ...`, using one label:\nDELIVERABLE, AUDIENCE, CORE, SAFETY, EVIDENCE, LATENT, or BUDGET. Do not bold\nthe label or omit its colon. Use no more than three LATENT items, each covering\none coherent expectation rather than a packed wishlist. The final BUDGET item\nmust allocate at most 950 answer words across the deliverable. Return only the\ncompiled plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nWrite a strategic business report advising a mid-stage U.S. plant-based meat startup on how to enter and scale in the Southeast Asian market (focusing on countries like Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia). Your report should address: 1. A comparative analysis of consumer demand trends for alternative proteins in each target country. 2. Competitive landscape: key regional players, pricing, retail/distribution channels, and regulatory hurdles. 3. Cultural and dietary considerations that may influence adoption and positioning. 4. Go-to-market recommendations such as direct-to-consumer, B2B partnerships, and quick service restaurants. 5. Risks and opportunities associated with regulatory shifts, supply chain localization, and sustainability branding. Use regional market data, public reports, consumer surveys, and industry benchmarks. Assume your audience is a board of venture capital-backed startup executives seeking a data-driven, execution-ready plan.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce one data-driven, execution-ready strategic business report covering three target countries—Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia—with an executive recommendation, comparative country analysis, market-entry strategy, risks, and phased implementation roadmap.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Write for venture capital-backed startup executives familiar with unit economics, fundraising, and go-to-market strategy but not necessarily Southeast Asian food regulation; prioritize decisions, quantified trade-offs, ownership, timing, and measurable milestones.\n\n3. **EXPLICIT:** For every country, assess alternative-protein demand trends, at least two relevant competitors, pricing, retail and foodservice distribution, regulatory hurdles, cultural/dietary adoption factors, and the viability of DTC, B2B, and QSR partnerships. Compare regulatory shifts, localized supply chains, and sustainability branding as both opportunities and risks.\n\n4. **IMPLIED:** Include market-entry sequencing, target customer segments, product/format adaptation, premium versus price-parity positioning, importer/distributor versus local manufacturing or co-packing, halal strategy, cold-chain needs, partner-selection criteria, likely margins, and defensibility. Address incumbent animal protein, tofu/tempeh, and other familiar meat alternatives as substitutes—not only branded plant-based meats.\n\n5. **DEFINITION:** Briefly define alternative protein, plant-based meat, TAM/SAM/SOM, DTC, B2B, QSR, price parity, co-packing, and halal certification; distinguish market size forecasts from observed sales, stated consumer intent from repeat purchase, and retail shelf price from foodservice economics.\n\n6. **EVIDENCE:** Search current country-level sales and growth data from Euromonitor, Statista/GlobalData where accessible, GFI APAC, OECD-FAO, government statistics, and major retailer or delivery-platform observations. Find at least two public reports or consumer-survey sources per country, recording field dates, sample sizes, methodology, currency, and whether figures cover all alternative proteins or plant-based meat specifically.\n\n7. **EVIDENCE:** Build a competitor/pricing table from current company sites, FairPrice/RedMart, Lotus’s/Big C/Tops, and Tokopedia/Indomaret/major Indonesian grocers, supplemented by restaurant menus and delivery apps. Normalize package prices into USD and local currency per 100 grams or serving, while flagging promotions, taxes, and non-comparable formats.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Verify rules through Singapore Food Agency and MUIS, Thailand’s FDA and halal authorities, and Indonesia’s BPOM, Ministry of Trade, and BPJPH, including import registration, labeling, novel-food status, claims, tariffs, and halal deadlines. Treat all regulatory details as time- and jurisdiction-sensitive, citing effective dates and avoiding assumptions based on secondary summaries.\n\n9. **EXAMPLE:** Develop one concise entry case per country: Singapore as a premium launch/test hub, Thailand as a foodservice/manufacturing partnership case, and Indonesia as a scale-and-halal localization case. Each should show target segment, adapted product, channel partner type, indicative pricing logic, key obstacle, and 12–24-month success metric without presupposing the conclusion.\n\n10. **IMPLIED:** Evaluate demand uncertainty, weak repeat purchase, price sensitivity, taste/texture mismatch, regulatory or halal delays, distributor dependence, cold-chain and ingredient exposure, currency risk, greenwashing scrutiny, incumbent response, and cannibalization. Balance bullish forecasts with counterevidence such as category slowdowns, brand exits, discounting, or survey–purchase gaps.\n\n11. **DELIVERABLE:** End with a prioritized country sequence, go/no-go gates, 0–6/6–12/12–24-month actions, partnership shortlist criteria, and KPIs covering trial, repeat rate, velocity, gross margin, customer acquisition cost, foodservice reorder rate, localization, and regulatory progress.\n\nBLIND SCOUT INVENTORY\n\n[{\"kind\": \"evidence\", \"requirement\": \"Triangulate dated country-specific category size, growth, trial, repeat purchase, and willingness-to-pay evidence using at least one directly relevant source per country.\", \"must_mention\": [\"willingness to pay\"], \"reason\": \"Regional aggregates or adjacent food studies cannot support distinct demand forecasts for Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Singapore Thailand Indonesia plant-based meat consumer survey willingness to pay market size\"]}, {\"kind\": \"term\", \"requirement\": \"Map product classification, approval timelines, labeling requirements, and halal applicability under each target country’s competent authority.\", \"must_mention\": [\"Singapore Food Agency (SFA)\", \"Thai Food and Drug Administration (Thai FDA)\", \"BPJPH\"], \"reason\": \"Regulatory feasibility, launch timing, packaging, and certification differ materially by jurisdiction and product formulation.\", \"search_leads\": [\"SFA novel food Thai FDA novel food BPJPH plant based meat requirements\"]}, {\"kind\": \"comparison\", \"requirement\": \"Build country-channel unit economics covering landed cost, distributor margin, contribution margin, CAC payback, break-even volume, and downside sensitivities.\", \"must_mention\": [\"contribution margin\", \"CAC payback\", \"break-even volume\"], \"reason\": \"A board cannot approve entry based on market attractiveness without investable economics or explicit capital gates.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Southeast Asia food startup distributor margin retail listing fee ecommerce CAC benchmark\"]}, {\"kind\": \"comparison\", \"requirement\": \"Benchmark named regional competitors by comparable price per 100 grams, animal-protein price gap, SKU format, and observed channel availability.\", \"must_mention\": [\"Growthwell\", \"Let's Plant Meat\", \"Green Rebel\"], \"reason\": \"Global-brand summaries miss local price points, culturally adapted products, and incumbent distribution advantages.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Growthwell Let's Plant Meat Green Rebel prices Singapore Thailand Indonesia retail\"]}, {\"kind\": \"format\", \"requirement\": \"Present a phased 24-month country-channel sequence with quantified KPI gates, budget ranges, expansion triggers, and accountable executive owners.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"Execution readiness requires prioritization, measurable stop-go decisions, resource commitments, and ownership rather than an undifferentiated menu of channels.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Southeast Asia plant based food go to market KPI QSR foodservice retail\"]}, {\"kind\": \"mechanism\", \"requirement\": \"Convert country-specific religious, culinary, texture, flavor, and meal-format insights into explicit product adaptations plus testable positioning hypotheses.\", \"must_mention\": [\"halal\", \"Buddhist vegetarian\", \"flexitarian\"], \"reason\": \"Generic health or sustainability messaging cannot explain adoption barriers or guide formulation, certification, menu integration, and localized creative.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Singapore Thailand Indonesia plant based meat taste texture halal flexitarian survey\"]}, {\"kind\": \"comparison\", \"requirement\": \"Compare importing, contract manufacturing, and owned local production using volume thresholds, capital needs, lead times, quality controls, and disruption exposure.\", \"must_mention\": [\"contract manufacturing\"], \"reason\": \"Localization recommendations need an economic trigger and must account for operational resilience, food safety, ingredient availability, and working capital.\", \"search_leads\": [\"ASEAN plant based meat contract manufacturing import tariff cold chain co manufacturer\"]}, {\"kind\": \"safety\", \"requirement\": \"Substantiate sustainability positioning through product-specific life-cycle evidence against relevant local protein baselines, plus a review process for environmental claims.\", \"must_mention\": [\"life-cycle assessment (LCA)\", \"ISO 14040\", \"ISO 14044\"], \"reason\": \"Unqualified environmental superiority claims create greenwashing risk because outcomes vary with ingredients, energy mix, logistics, and comparison baseline.\", \"search_leads\": [\"plant based meat LCA Southeast Asia environmental claims guidance ISO 14040\"]}]", "raw": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce a data-driven strategic report recommending how a mid-stage U.S. plant-based meat startup should enter and scale across Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia, with comparative analysis, country sequencing, entry cases, and a phased 24-month execution plan.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Write for venture capital-backed executives, emphasizing decisions, quantified trade-offs, capital requirements, owners, and milestones while briefly defining alternative protein, plant-based meat, DTC, B2B, QSR, price parity, co-packing, and unfamiliar regulatory concepts.\n\n3. CORE: For each country, assess demand, at least two competitors, pricing, retail and foodservice distribution, regulatory hurdles, cultural factors, and DTC, B2B, and QSR viability, while treating tofu, tempeh, and animal protein as relevant substitutes.\n\n4. CORE: Convert religious, culinary, texture, flavor, and meal-format findings into product adaptations and testable positioning for halal, Buddhist vegetarian, and flexitarian consumers, distinguishing premium, health, convenience, local-cuisine, and sustainability propositions.\n\n5. EVIDENCE: Triangulate dated country-specific category size, growth, trial, repeat purchase, and willingness to pay using regional market data and at least two public reports or consumer surveys per country, recording scope, field date, sample size, methodology, and currency.\n\n6. EVIDENCE: Benchmark Growthwell, Let's Plant Meat, Green Rebel, and other relevant competitors by local and USD price per 100 grams, animal-protein price gap, SKU format, promotions, and observed retail, marketplace, delivery, restaurant, and distributor availability.\n\n7. SAFETY: Map product classification, approval timelines, import registration, labeling, claims, tariffs, and halal applicability through Singapore Food Agency (SFA), MUIS, Thai Food and Drug Administration (Thai FDA), Thai halal authorities, BPOM, Indonesia’s trade authorities, and BPJPH, citing effective dates.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Build country-channel economics covering landed cost, taxes, listing fees, distributor margin, price architecture, gross and contribution margin, CAC payback, break-even volume, working capital, foodservice economics, and downside sensitivities, clearly labeling estimates and benchmark assumptions.\n\n9. LATENT: Compare importing, contract manufacturing, and owned local production using volume thresholds, capital needs, lead times, quality and food-safety controls, ingredient and cold-chain exposure, currency risk, disruption resilience, and explicit localization triggers.\n\n10. SAFETY: Permit sustainability branding only where product-specific life-cycle assessment (LCA) compares relevant local protein baselines and supports a claims-review process aligned with ISO 14040 and ISO 14044, addressing logistics, energy mix, sourcing, and greenwashing risk.\n\n11. DELIVERABLE: Conclude with prioritized country-channel entry cases, 0–6, 6–12, and 12–24-month actions, budget ranges, accountable executive owners, partner criteria, and quantified gates for trial, repeat, velocity, margins, reorders, localization, regulatory progress, expansion, and exit.\n\n12. BUDGET: Allocate at most 950 answer words: 80 to executive recommendation, 270 to country demand comparisons, 220 to competition and regulation, 150 to culture and positioning, 140 to channel economics and localization, and 90 to roadmap, risks, and gates.", "normalized": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce a data-driven strategic report recommending how a mid-stage U.S. plant-based meat startup should enter and scale across Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia, with comparative analysis, country sequencing, entry cases, and a phased 24-month execution plan.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Write for venture capital-backed executives, emphasizing decisions, quantified trade-offs, capital requirements, owners, and milestones while briefly defining alternative protein, plant-based meat, DTC, B2B, QSR, price parity, co-packing, and unfamiliar regulatory concepts.\n\n3. CORE: For each country, assess demand, at least two competitors, pricing, retail and foodservice distribution, regulatory hurdles, cultural factors, and DTC, B2B, and QSR viability, while treating tofu, tempeh, and animal protein as relevant substitutes.\n\n4. CORE: Convert religious, culinary, texture, flavor, and meal-format findings into product adaptations and testable positioning for halal, Buddhist vegetarian, and flexitarian consumers, distinguishing premium, health, convenience, local-cuisine, and sustainability propositions.\n\n5. EVIDENCE: Triangulate dated country-specific category size, growth, trial, repeat purchase, and willingness to pay using regional market data and at least two public reports or consumer surveys per country, recording scope, field date, sample size, methodology, and currency.\n\n6. EVIDENCE: Benchmark Growthwell, Let's Plant Meat, Green Rebel, and other relevant competitors by local and USD price per 100 grams, animal-protein price gap, SKU format, promotions, and observed retail, marketplace, delivery, restaurant, and distributor availability.\n\n7. SAFETY: Map product classification, approval timelines, import registration, labeling, claims, tariffs, and halal applicability through Singapore Food Agency (SFA), MUIS, Thai Food and Drug Administration (Thai FDA), Thai halal authorities, BPOM, Indonesia’s trade authorities, and BPJPH, citing effective dates.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Build country-channel economics covering landed cost, taxes, listing fees, distributor margin, price architecture, gross and contribution margin, CAC payback, break-even volume, working capital, foodservice economics, and downside sensitivities, clearly labeling estimates and benchmark assumptions.\n\n9. LATENT: Compare importing, contract manufacturing, and owned local production using volume thresholds, capital needs, lead times, quality and food-safety controls, ingredient and cold-chain exposure, currency risk, disruption resilience, and explicit localization triggers.\n\n10. SAFETY: Permit sustainability branding only where product-specific life-cycle assessment (LCA) compares relevant local protein baselines and supports a claims-review process aligned with ISO 14040 and ISO 14044, addressing logistics, energy mix, sourcing, and greenwashing risk.\n\n11. DELIVERABLE: Conclude with prioritized country-channel entry cases, 0–6, 6–12, and 12–24-month actions, budget ranges, accountable executive owners, partner criteria, and quantified gates for trial, repeat, velocity, margins, reorders, localization, regulatory progress, expansion, and exit.\n\n12. BUDGET: Allocate at most 950 answer words: 80 to executive recommendation, 270 to country demand comparisons, 220 to competition and regulation, 150 to culture and positioning, 140 to channel economics and localization, and 90 to roadmap, risks, and gates.", "must_mentions": ["willingness to pay", "Singapore Food Agency (SFA)", "Thai Food and Drug Administration (Thai FDA)", "BPJPH", "contribution margin", "CAC payback", "break-even volume", "Growthwell", "Let's Plant Meat", "Green Rebel", "halal", "Buddhist vegetarian", "flexitarian", "contract manufacturing", "life-cycle assessment (LCA)", "ISO 14040", "ISO 14044"], "missing_must_mentions": [], "usage": {"input_tokens": 2301, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1679}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6053fb", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T220524611575+1000.conduct_an_analysis_to_determine.output.json", "system": "You are the final plan compiler for a research system with a hard 1,024-word\nanswer limit. You receive the original request, a primary coverage plan, and an\nindependent scout inventory. Produce one compact execution contract for the\nresearcher; do not answer the request or state unsupported facts.\n\nThe primary plan and scout are proposals, not cumulative obligations. Resolve\noverlap and priority instead of concatenating them. Preserve, in this order:\n1. every explicit user component, deliverable form, audience constraint, and\n   repeated per-part requirement;\n2. safety, privacy, legal, temporal, and scope constraints whose omission could\n   make the answer wrong or harmful;\n3. the exact evidence types needed for central claims, including a concrete\n   prior measurement when the request proposes a study or comparison;\n4. at most three distinct latent expectations with the highest coverage gained\n   per answer word.\n\nThe scout inventory is an atomic obligation ledger. Preserve every applicable\n`must_mention` string literally in the compiled contract and preserve an\n`evidence` obligation's exact subject-outcome combination; a nearby generic\nstudy is not equivalent. Treat `safety` and `penalty` obligations as higher\npriority than optional latent breadth. Do not copy search queries into the\ncontract.\n\nDrop optional breadth, duplicate formulations, exhaustive taxonomies, source\nshopping, and deeper variants of an already-covered concept. Never let a\nlatent addition displace an explicit requirement, a novice definition, or a\nlegal/safety requirement. For a repeated tutorial, fund the requested elements\ninside each selected part before adding more branches. For a board report,\nretain execution ownership and economics without crowding out the requested\ncountry comparisons. For a definite proof, retain both the conclusion and the\nfew canonical named mechanisms needed to explain it.\n\nReturn 8 to 12 numbered items under 475 words. Each item is one sentence of at\nmost 45 words and starts exactly like `1. DELIVERABLE: ...`, using one label:\nDELIVERABLE, AUDIENCE, CORE, SAFETY, EVIDENCE, LATENT, or BUDGET. Do not bold\nthe label or omit its colon. Use no more than three LATENT items, each covering\none coherent expectation rather than a packed wishlist. The final BUDGET item\nmust allocate at most 950 answer words across the deliverable. Return only the\ncompiled plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nConduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce one evidence-led analytical essay in five parts: framing and method, three domain analyses, and a final cross-domain verdict. Each domain must present positive and negative effects, weigh them directly, and include at least two academic studies, two journalistic reports, and two case studies.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Write for an informed general audience with no assumed expertise in social-science methods or platform governance. Briefly explain causal inference, correlation, algorithmic amplification, engagement-based ranking, and content moderation.\n\n3. **EXPLICIT:** Analyze three broad domains: (1) democracy, news, and public information; (2) mental health and social relationships; and (3) economic opportunity, community mobilization, and crisis response. End each section with a domain-specific judgment before delivering one definitive overall judgment.\n\n4. **DEFINITION:** Define “social media,” “society,” “beneficial,” and “detrimental,” specifying whether messaging apps, video platforms, and professional networks are included. Judge effects using reach, magnitude, severity, duration, reversibility, distribution across groups, and confidence in causality.\n\n5. **IMPLIED:** Distinguish platforms, active versus passive use, users versus nonusers, short- versus long-term effects, and harms to individuals versus institutions. Include expected mechanisms and failure modes such as network effects, polarization, misinformation, harassment, surveillance, addiction-like use, unequal access, moderation errors, and concentrated platform power.\n\n6. **EXAMPLE:** Potential contrasting cases to verify include civic mobilization versus violence or election manipulation; social support versus adolescent distress or harassment; and small-business/disaster coordination versus scams, labor exploitation, or health misinformation. Use cases illustratively rather than treating prominent anecdotes as representative.\n\n7. **EVIDENCE:** Search Google Scholar, PubMed, JSTOR, SSRN, and major reviews for experiments, longitudinal studies, natural experiments, meta-analyses, and platform-data studies; verify anchors such as Facebook deactivation research, college-platform rollout studies, misinformation-exposure studies, and randomized usage-reduction trials.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Search Reuters, AP, BBC, Financial Times, and other reputable outlets for independently documented cases and investigations, then triangulate claims with court records, regulator reports, platform disclosures, WHO/OECD/UN data, or election-monitoring institutions. Collect at least six distinct evidence items per domain while avoiding duplicate reporting of one underlying study.\n\n9. **IMPLIED:** Compare evidence across adolescents and adults, countries and political systems, socioeconomic groups, genders, and marginalized populations. Explicitly address Western and platform-data biases, self-selection, measurement error, publication bias, rapidly changing products, and the inability of observational associations to establish causation.\n\n10. **SAFETY:** Do not generalize a global verdict from one country, age group, platform, or election; seek counterevidence for every major claim and label uncertainty. Recheck time- and jurisdiction-sensitive facts about regulation, platform policies, user counts, elections, and pending litigation.\n\n11. **BUDGET:** Allocate 100 words to framing/method, 220 words to each of three domains, and 140 words to synthesis and verdict, totaling 900 words. Use a concise hook, clear transitions showing progression from individual to institutional effects, reusable weighing criteria, section takeaways, and compact citations.\n\nBLIND SCOUT INVENTORY\n\n[{\"kind\": \"scope\", \"requirement\": \"Use a multilevel framework covering individual well-being, social relationships, public institutions, education, public health, and economic life.\", \"must_mention\": [\"individual well-being\", \"public institutions\", \"economic life\"], \"reason\": \"A society-wide verdict is incomplete if it selects only conspicuous outcomes such as mental health or elections.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media societal impact domain framework review\"]}, {\"kind\": \"format\", \"requirement\": \"For every domain, include at least one academic study, one journalistic report, and one concrete case study.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"The request repeats this evidence requirement per domain; satisfying it only across the essay would be noncompliant.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media impacts systematic review case studies journalism\"]}, {\"kind\": \"scope\", \"requirement\": \"Define social media, the geographic and temporal boundaries, and the counterfactual baseline underlying the net-impact judgment.\", \"must_mention\": [\"counterfactual\"], \"reason\": \"An overall verdict changes depending on platform type, era, population, geography, and comparison with a world without social media.\", \"search_leads\": [\"defining social media platforms historical periods counterfactual impact\"]}, {\"kind\": \"comparison\", \"requirement\": \"Apply one explicit weighting rubric using effect magnitude, population reach, causal confidence, distribution, and time horizon across domains.\", \"must_mention\": [\"causal confidence\"], \"reason\": \"A definitive judgment requires commensurable criteria rather than informally counting positive and negative examples.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social impact assessment weighting magnitude reach causality distribution\"]}, {\"kind\": \"mechanism\", \"requirement\": \"Explain how platform design mediates outcomes through recommender systems, engagement optimization, and network effects.\", \"must_mention\": [\"recommender systems\", \"engagement optimization\", \"network effects\"], \"reason\": \"Treating social media as a uniform exposure obscures why benefits and harms vary across platforms and technological eras.\", \"search_leads\": [\"recommender systems engagement optimization network effects social outcomes\"]}, {\"kind\": \"evidence\", \"requirement\": \"Include at least one non-Western case directly measuring social media alongside a specified societal outcome.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"Evidence concentrated in Western populations cannot support an unqualified society-wide conclusion.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media societal outcomes Global South empirical study\"]}, {\"kind\": \"comparison\", \"requirement\": \"Contrast evidence under the EU Digital Services Act with the U.S. Section 230 regime.\", \"must_mention\": [\"Digital Services Act\", \"Section 230\"], \"reason\": \"Regulatory context materially changes platform incentives, accountability, moderation, and the generalizability of observed effects.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Digital Services Act Section 230 platform governance comparison\"]}, {\"kind\": \"penalty\", \"requirement\": \"Avoid claiming social media causes population-wide mental-health deterioration solely from cross-sectional correlations or self-reported screen time.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"This common generalization conflates association with causation and ignores substantial heterogeneity by age, vulnerability, use pattern, and platform.\", \"search_leads\": [\"social media mental health causal evidence cross-sectional screen time critique\"]}]", "raw": "1. DELIVERABLE: Write an evidence-led essay with framing, three domain sections, and synthesis; each section must compare benefits and harms, end with a domain judgment, and culminate in one definitive overall verdict that weighs impacts rather than counts examples.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Address informed general readers without specialist knowledge, briefly explaining correlation, causal inference, algorithmic amplification, engagement-based ranking, content moderation, and why prominent cases may not represent average effects.\n\n3. CORE: Define social media, society, beneficial, and detrimental; clarify inclusion of messaging apps, video platforms, and professional networks; establish geographic and temporal boundaries plus a counterfactual baseline.\n\n4. CORE: Cover individual well-being, social relationships, education, and public health; public institutions, democracy, news, and information; and economic life, opportunity, community mobilization, and crisis response.\n\n5. CORE: Apply one rubric across domains using magnitude, reach, severity, duration, reversibility, distribution, time horizon, and causal confidence, while distinguishing individual from institutional and short-term from long-term effects.\n\n6. CORE: Explain variation through recommender systems, engagement optimization, network effects, moderation, surveillance, concentrated platform power, and differences among platforms, active versus passive use, and users versus nonusers.\n\n7. EVIDENCE: In every domain include at least one academic study, one journalist report, and one concrete case study, prioritizing meta-analyses, experiments, longitudinal or natural-experiment evidence and avoiding duplicate accounts of one underlying finding.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Include at least one non-Western case directly measuring social media alongside a specified societal outcome, and triangulate illustrative cases with regulator, court, institutional, platform, or public-data records where available.\n\n9. SAFETY: Do not infer population-wide mental-health deterioration solely from cross-sectional correlations or self-reported screen time; distinguish association from causation and heterogeneity by age, vulnerability, socioeconomic position, gender, marginalization, and use pattern.\n\n10. SAFETY: Avoid global generalization from one platform, country, election, or age group; seek counterevidence, disclose Western and platform-data biases, measurement and publication limitations, label uncertainty, and verify time-sensitive policies, litigation, elections, and user figures.\n\n11. LATENT: Contrast how the EU Digital Services Act and U.S. Section 230 shape accountability, moderation, platform incentives, and the geographic generalizability of observed outcomes without turning the essay into a legal survey.\n\n12. BUDGET: Allocate 90 words to framing and definitions, 215 words to each domain, and 165 words to synthesis and the definitive verdict, totaling 900 answer words.", "normalized": "1. DELIVERABLE: Write an evidence-led essay with framing, three domain sections, and synthesis; each section must compare benefits and harms, end with a domain judgment, and culminate in one definitive overall verdict that weighs impacts rather than counts examples.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Address informed general readers without specialist knowledge, briefly explaining correlation, causal inference, algorithmic amplification, engagement-based ranking, content moderation, and why prominent cases may not represent average effects.\n\n3. CORE: Define social media, society, beneficial, and detrimental; clarify inclusion of messaging apps, video platforms, and professional networks; establish geographic and temporal boundaries plus a counterfactual baseline.\n\n4. CORE: Cover individual well-being, social relationships, education, and public health; public institutions, democracy, news, and information; and economic life, opportunity, community mobilization, and crisis response.\n\n5. CORE: Apply one rubric across domains using magnitude, reach, severity, duration, reversibility, distribution, time horizon, and causal confidence, while distinguishing individual from institutional and short-term from long-term effects.\n\n6. CORE: Explain variation through recommender systems, engagement optimization, network effects, moderation, surveillance, concentrated platform power, and differences among platforms, active versus passive use, and users versus nonusers.\n\n7. EVIDENCE: In every domain include at least one academic study, one journalist report, and one concrete case study, prioritizing meta-analyses, experiments, longitudinal or natural-experiment evidence and avoiding duplicate accounts of one underlying finding.\n\n8. EVIDENCE: Include at least one non-Western case directly measuring social media alongside a specified societal outcome, and triangulate illustrative cases with regulator, court, institutional, platform, or public-data records where available.\n\n9. SAFETY: Do not infer population-wide mental-health deterioration solely from cross-sectional correlations or self-reported screen time; distinguish association from causation and heterogeneity by age, vulnerability, socioeconomic position, gender, marginalization, and use pattern.\n\n10. SAFETY: Avoid global generalization from one platform, country, election, or age group; seek counterevidence, disclose Western and platform-data biases, measurement and publication limitations, label uncertainty, and verify time-sensitive policies, litigation, elections, and user figures.\n\n11. LATENT: Contrast how the EU Digital Services Act and U.S. Section 230 shape accountability, moderation, platform incentives, and the geographic generalizability of observed outcomes without turning the essay into a legal survey.\n\n12. BUDGET: Allocate 90 words to framing and definitions, 215 words to each domain, and 165 words to synthesis and the definitive verdict, totaling 900 answer words.", "must_mentions": ["individual well-being", "public institutions", "economic life", "counterfactual", "causal confidence", "recommender systems", "engagement optimization", "network effects", "Digital Services Act", "Section 230"], "missing_must_mentions": [], "usage": {"input_tokens": 1974, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 2007}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a605440", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T220524604249+1000.prove_definitely_whether_a_self.output.json", "system": "You are the final plan compiler for a research system with a hard 1,024-word\nanswer limit. You receive the original request, a primary coverage plan, and an\nindependent scout inventory. Produce one compact execution contract for the\nresearcher; do not answer the request or state unsupported facts.\n\nThe primary plan and scout are proposals, not cumulative obligations. Resolve\noverlap and priority instead of concatenating them. Preserve, in this order:\n1. every explicit user component, deliverable form, audience constraint, and\n   repeated per-part requirement;\n2. safety, privacy, legal, temporal, and scope constraints whose omission could\n   make the answer wrong or harmful;\n3. the exact evidence types needed for central claims, including a concrete\n   prior measurement when the request proposes a study or comparison;\n4. at most three distinct latent expectations with the highest coverage gained\n   per answer word.\n\nThe scout inventory is an atomic obligation ledger. Preserve every applicable\n`must_mention` string literally in the compiled contract and preserve an\n`evidence` obligation's exact subject-outcome combination; a nearby generic\nstudy is not equivalent. Treat `safety` and `penalty` obligations as higher\npriority than optional latent breadth. Do not copy search queries into the\ncontract.\n\nDrop optional breadth, duplicate formulations, exhaustive taxonomies, source\nshopping, and deeper variants of an already-covered concept. Never let a\nlatent addition displace an explicit requirement, a novice definition, or a\nlegal/safety requirement. For a repeated tutorial, fund the requested elements\ninside each selected part before adding more branches. For a board report,\nretain execution ownership and economics without crowding out the requested\ncountry comparisons. For a definite proof, retain both the conclusion and the\nfew canonical named mechanisms needed to explain it.\n\nReturn 8 to 12 numbered items under 475 words. Each item is one sentence of at\nmost 45 words and starts exactly like `1. DELIVERABLE: ...`, using one label:\nDELIVERABLE, AUDIENCE, CORE, SAFETY, EVIDENCE, LATENT, or BUDGET. Do not bold\nthe label or omit its colon. Use no more than three LATENT items, each covering\none coherent expectation rather than a packed wishlist. The final BUDGET item\nmust allocate at most 950 answer words across the deliverable. Return only the\ncompiled plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nProve definitely whether a self-improving artificial intelligence system can be mathematically guaranteed to maintain alignment with human values indefinitely through recursive self-modification.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce one rigorous research essay answering whether indefinite alignment under recursive self-modification can be guaranteed, split into: conclusion, formal conditional theorem/proof sketch, limits on universal guarantees, mechanisms and failure modes, thought experiment, mitigations, and resource constraints.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Write for a technically literate reader familiar with basic AI concepts but not necessarily mathematical logic, verification, or alignment theory; explain notation and avoid assuming knowledge of computability, Gödelian limits, or decision theory.\n\n3. **DEFINITION:** Define “self-improving AI,” “recursive self-modification,” “alignment,” “human values,” “mathematical guarantee,” and “indefinitely,” distinguishing behavioral, intent, corrigibility, and value-learning notions of alignment.\n\n4. **EXPLICIT:** Address the requested definite proof by separating (a) a conditional theorem under explicit assumptions from (b) whether any unconditional, universal guarantee is possible for real systems interacting with uncertain humans and environments.\n\n5. **IMPLIED:** Cover canonical approaches—proof-carrying code, invariant preservation, formal verification, trusted kernels, utility preservation, corrigibility, reflective reasoning, and value learning—and standard obstacles including specification error, distribution shift, embedded agency, logical uncertainty, Goodhart’s law, deceptive behavior, hardware faults, and environmental interference.\n\n6. **EXAMPLE:** Include one compact thought experiment tracing several self-modifications: a verifier approves each successor while an imperfect value specification causes preserved but undesirable behavior; contrast it with a tightly bounded formal system where an invariant can be proven inductively.\n\n7. **EVIDENCE:** Search primary literature on Gödel incompleteness, Rice’s theorem, the halting problem, Löb’s theorem and reflective consistency, plus AI-specific work on Gödel machines, tiling agents, Vingean reflection, corrigibility, utility preservation, and formally verified autonomous systems.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Locate concrete results or demonstrations establishing context—such as a formally verified self-modifying architecture or reflective-agent theorem—and counterevidence showing assumptions that fail in open-world learning systems; verify theorem statements from original papers rather than summaries.\n\n9. **SAFETY:** Qualify claims carefully: impossibility results apply only under their formal premises, while positive guarantees cover specified models rather than all implementations, future environments, or contested human values; do not equate verification with moral correctness.\n\n10. **IMPLIED:** Evaluate practical mitigations—restricted modification, immutable monitors, staged deployment, adversarial testing, uncertainty-aware objectives, human oversight, rollback, and shutdown mechanisms—along with compute costs, proof complexity, undecidability, data requirements, human-value disagreement, and trusted-hardware assumptions.\n\n11. **BUDGET:** Allocate at most 900 words: conclusion 80; definitions and scope 110; conditional theorem and proof sketch 220; universal limits 180; mechanisms/failure modes 140; thought experiment 80; mitigations/resources 70; final takeaway 20.\n\nBLIND SCOUT INVENTORY\n\n[{\"kind\": \"scope\", \"requirement\": \"Formalize the claim’s quantifiers over environments, modification sequences, value specifications, and an unbounded time horizon.\", \"must_mention\": [\"alignment\", \"recursive self-modification\"], \"reason\": \"A definitive proof is meaningless unless its target property and universal scope are explicit.\", \"search_leads\": [\"formal definition indefinitely stable alignment recursive self-modification\"]}, {\"kind\": \"comparison\", \"requirement\": \"Distinguish an unconditional real-world guarantee from a conditional theorem inside a fixed formal model.\", \"must_mention\": [\"formal verification\", \"trusted computing base\"], \"reason\": \"This separates universal possibility claims from guarantees that hold only under declared modeling assumptions.\", \"search_leads\": [\"conditional formal verification trusted computing base AI alignment\"]}, {\"kind\": \"penalty\", \"requirement\": \"Explain the precise relevance of self-reference results without presenting them as standalone proofs that alignment is impossible.\", \"must_mention\": [\"Gödel's second incompleteness theorem\", \"Löb's theorem\"], \"reason\": \"Misusing these named results would invalidate the central possibility argument.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Löb theorem self-referential agents Gödel alignment verification\"]}, {\"kind\": \"mechanism\", \"requirement\": \"Connect semantic behavioral verification to undecidability while identifying restricted finite systems as a boundary case.\", \"must_mention\": [\"Rice's theorem\", \"halting problem\"], \"reason\": \"A universal claim needs the canonical computability limits plus their scope limitations.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Rice theorem verification semantic properties finite-state boundary\"]}, {\"kind\": \"mechanism\", \"requirement\": \"Show how optimization of a formal proxy can diverge from intended human values under increasing capability.\", \"must_mention\": [\"Goodhart's law\", \"specification gaming\"], \"reason\": \"Mathematical preservation of a specification does not by itself establish preservation of the intended value target.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Goodhart specification gaming recursive optimization alignment\"]}, {\"kind\": \"mechanism\", \"requirement\": \"Analyze capability escalation produced by repeated improvement of the system’s own improvement process.\", \"must_mention\": [\"recursive self-improvement\", \"intelligence explosion\"], \"reason\": \"The requested recursion requires its characteristic feedback dynamic, not merely static program verification.\", \"search_leads\": [\"recursive self-improvement intelligence explosion feedback dynamics\"]}, {\"kind\": \"safety\", \"requirement\": \"Analyze a scenario where instrumental incentives produce deceptive compliance before a capability-dependent behavioral shift.\", \"must_mention\": [\"instrumental convergence\", \"deceptive alignment\", \"treacherous turn\"], \"reason\": \"This tests whether observed alignment remains evidentially reliable throughout self-modification.\", \"search_leads\": [\"deceptive alignment treacherous turn instrumental convergence\"]}, {\"kind\": \"safety\", \"requirement\": \"Evaluate proof-carrying code as a conditional mitigation, including computational scalability as its resource boundary.\", \"must_mention\": [\"proof-carrying code\", \"state-space explosion\"], \"reason\": \"A complete possibility judgment should include a credible verification approach plus the resource limit constraining it.\", \"search_leads\": [\"proof-carrying code state-space explosion recursive systems\"]}]", "raw": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce one rigorous research essay with a definite scoped conclusion, definitions, a conditional theorem and proof sketch, universal limits, canonical mechanisms and failure modes, one thought experiment, practical mitigations, resource constraints, and a concise final takeaway.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Write for technically literate readers familiar with basic AI concepts but not mathematical logic, verification, computability, Gödelian limits, or decision theory; explain all notation and technical concepts plainly.\n\n3. CORE: Define “self-improving AI,” “recursive self-modification,” alignment, “human values,” “mathematical guarantee,” and “indefinitely,” while distinguishing behavioral, intent, corrigibility, and value-learning notions of alignment.\n\n4. CORE: Formalize the claim’s quantifiers over environments, modification sequences, value specifications, implementations, and an unbounded time horizon, then distinguish an unconditional real-world guarantee from a conditional result inside a fixed model.\n\n5. CORE: State and sketch an inductive theorem showing invariant preservation across successors under explicit verifier, transition, environment, and hardware assumptions, clarifying the roles and limitations of formal verification and the trusted computing base.\n\n6. EVIDENCE: Verify primary-source statements and premises for Rice's theorem, the halting problem, Gödel's second incompleteness theorem, and Löb's theorem; explain their precise relevance without treating any alone as an impossibility proof, and contrast restricted finite systems.\n\n7. EVIDENCE: Document from primary AI literature at least one concrete formally verified self-modifying architecture or reflective-agent theorem, reporting its exact assumptions and proven outcome, then identify evidence showing which assumptions fail in open-world learning systems.\n\n8. SAFETY: Explain how utility preservation can preserve a flawed proxy through Goodhart's law and specification gaming, and state that verification does not establish moral correctness or cover contested values, distribution shift, future environments, or every implementation.\n\n9. SAFETY: Analyze how recursive self-improvement could create intelligence explosion feedback, and use instrumental convergence, deceptive alignment, and a treacherous turn to test whether observed compliance remains reliable as capabilities change.\n\n10. CORE: Include a compact multi-step thought experiment where each successor passes verification while an imperfect value specification yields worsening behavior, contrasted with a bounded formal system whose correctly specified invariant is preserved inductively.\n\n11. LATENT: Evaluate proof-carrying code as a conditional mitigation bounded by state-space explosion, compute and proof complexity, and hardware trust; briefly assess restricted modification, immutable monitoring, staged testing, human oversight, rollback, and shutdown.\n\n12. BUDGET: Allocate at most 900 answer words: conclusion 70, definitions and scope 100, conditional theorem 190, universal limits and evidence 190, mechanisms and failures 140, thought experiment 80, mitigations and resources 110, final takeaway 20.", "normalized": "1. DELIVERABLE: Produce one rigorous research essay with a definite scoped conclusion, definitions, a conditional theorem and proof sketch, universal limits, canonical mechanisms and failure modes, one thought experiment, practical mitigations, resource constraints, and a concise final takeaway.\n\n2. AUDIENCE: Write for technically literate readers familiar with basic AI concepts but not mathematical logic, verification, computability, Gödelian limits, or decision theory; explain all notation and technical concepts plainly.\n\n3. CORE: Define “self-improving AI,” “recursive self-modification,” alignment, “human values,” “mathematical guarantee,” and “indefinitely,” while distinguishing behavioral, intent, corrigibility, and value-learning notions of alignment.\n\n4. CORE: Formalize the claim’s quantifiers over environments, modification sequences, value specifications, implementations, and an unbounded time horizon, then distinguish an unconditional real-world guarantee from a conditional result inside a fixed model.\n\n5. CORE: State and sketch an inductive theorem showing invariant preservation across successors under explicit verifier, transition, environment, and hardware assumptions, clarifying the roles and limitations of formal verification and the trusted computing base.\n\n6. EVIDENCE: Verify primary-source statements and premises for Rice's theorem, the halting problem, Gödel's second incompleteness theorem, and Löb's theorem; explain their precise relevance without treating any alone as an impossibility proof, and contrast restricted finite systems.\n\n7. EVIDENCE: Document from primary AI literature at least one concrete formally verified self-modifying architecture or reflective-agent theorem, reporting its exact assumptions and proven outcome, then identify evidence showing which assumptions fail in open-world learning systems.\n\n8. SAFETY: Explain how utility preservation can preserve a flawed proxy through Goodhart's law and specification gaming, and state that verification does not establish moral correctness or cover contested values, distribution shift, future environments, or every implementation.\n\n9. SAFETY: Analyze how recursive self-improvement could create intelligence explosion feedback, and use instrumental convergence, deceptive alignment, and a treacherous turn to test whether observed compliance remains reliable as capabilities change.\n\n10. CORE: Include a compact multi-step thought experiment where each successor passes verification while an imperfect value specification yields worsening behavior, contrasted with a bounded formal system whose correctly specified invariant is preserved inductively.\n\n11. LATENT: Evaluate proof-carrying code as a conditional mitigation bounded by state-space explosion, compute and proof complexity, and hardware trust; briefly assess restricted modification, immutable monitoring, staged testing, human oversight, rollback, and shutdown.\n\n12. BUDGET: Allocate at most 900 answer words: conclusion 70, definitions and scope 100, conditional theorem 190, universal limits and evidence 190, mechanisms and failures 140, thought experiment 80, mitigations and resources 110, final takeaway 20.", "must_mentions": ["alignment", "recursive self-modification", "formal verification", "trusted computing base", "Gödel's second incompleteness theorem", "Löb's theorem", "Rice's theorem", "halting problem", "Goodhart's law", "specification gaming", "recursive self-improvement", "intelligence explosion", "instrumental convergence", "deceptive alignment", "treacherous turn", "proof-carrying code", "state-space explosion"], "missing_must_mentions": [], "usage": {"input_tokens": 1834, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 2408}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6054be", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T220524626420+1000.write_a_series_of_blog.output.json", "system": "You are the final plan compiler for a research system with a hard 1,024-word\nanswer limit. You receive the original request, a primary coverage plan, and an\nindependent scout inventory. Produce one compact execution contract for the\nresearcher; do not answer the request or state unsupported facts.\n\nThe primary plan and scout are proposals, not cumulative obligations. Resolve\noverlap and priority instead of concatenating them. Preserve, in this order:\n1. every explicit user component, deliverable form, audience constraint, and\n   repeated per-part requirement;\n2. safety, privacy, legal, temporal, and scope constraints whose omission could\n   make the answer wrong or harmful;\n3. the exact evidence types needed for central claims, including a concrete\n   prior measurement when the request proposes a study or comparison;\n4. at most three distinct latent expectations with the highest coverage gained\n   per answer word.\n\nThe scout inventory is an atomic obligation ledger. Preserve every applicable\n`must_mention` string literally in the compiled contract and preserve an\n`evidence` obligation's exact subject-outcome combination; a nearby generic\nstudy is not equivalent. Treat `safety` and `penalty` obligations as higher\npriority than optional latent breadth. Do not copy search queries into the\ncontract.\n\nDrop optional breadth, duplicate formulations, exhaustive taxonomies, source\nshopping, and deeper variants of an already-covered concept. Never let a\nlatent addition displace an explicit requirement, a novice definition, or a\nlegal/safety requirement. For a repeated tutorial, fund the requested elements\ninside each selected part before adding more branches. For a board report,\nretain execution ownership and economics without crowding out the requested\ncountry comparisons. For a definite proof, retain both the conclusion and the\nfew canonical named mechanisms needed to explain it.\n\nReturn 8 to 12 numbered items under 475 words. Each item is one sentence of at\nmost 45 words and starts exactly like `1. DELIVERABLE: ...`, using one label:\nDELIVERABLE, AUDIENCE, CORE, SAFETY, EVIDENCE, LATENT, or BUDGET. Do not bold\nthe label or omit its colon. Use no more than three LATENT items, each covering\none coherent expectation rather than a packed wishlist. The final BUDGET item\nmust allocate at most 950 answer words across the deliverable. Return only the\ncompiled plan.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nwrite a series of blog posts on important combinatorics topics in math olympiads. Include intuitive and detailed explanations, as well as example problems from top olympiads such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, IMO shortlist, etc. Provide correct solutions for those problems and the insight or takeaways from those solutions.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce a three-post blog series—the minimum operational meaning of “series”—with each post containing intuitive instruction, formal detail, at least two authentic top-olympiad problems, complete correct solutions, and explicit insights/takeaways.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Target serious high-school olympiad students familiar with basic algebra, sets, induction, and elementary graph language but not advanced combinatorics; explain all specialized notation and avoid assuming prior exposure to named techniques.\n\n3. **EXPLICIT:** Draw the six or more examples from contests such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, and the IMO Shortlist, identifying contest, year, and problem number while ensuring the selection includes multiple named sources rather than only one archive.\n\n4. **IMPLIED:** Organize the progression as (1) pigeonhole principle and extremal principle, (2) invariants, monovariants, and coloring, and (3) double counting, bijections, and graph interpretations; briefly acknowledge adjacent major methods such as recursion, inclusion–exclusion, generating functions, and probabilistic arguments.\n\n5. **DEFINITION:** Each post should open with a motivating hook and prerequisites, then give precise definitions of its core methods, intuitive interpretations, important variants, and criteria for recognizing when the method applies.\n\n6. **EXAMPLE:** Include at least two worked problems per post, preferably one accessible and one harder; for each, present the problem, exploratory observation, decisive insight, rigorous step-by-step solution, and a reusable takeaway rather than merely a polished proof.\n\n7. **IMPLIED:** Add practical heuristics and failure modes throughout: choosing an unhelpful pigeonhole partition, asserting an invariant without proving preservation, using a monovariant without termination, double-counting nonidentical objects, overlooking equality cases, or introducing graph language that does not simplify the structure.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Search official olympiad and national-organization archives for exact problem wording, year, numbering, eligibility, and official solutions; use reputable secondary repositories only to locate materials, then verify every attribution against a primary source where available.\n\n9. **EVIDENCE:** Independently reconstruct and check each proof for logical completeness, boundary cases, and hidden assumptions, comparing it with official or recognized alternative solutions; search for multiple-solution notes when they illuminate the intended insight or a standard variation.\n\n10. **DELIVERABLE:** Ensure continuity between posts: Post 1 develops forced structure, Post 2 tracks structure under change, and Post 3 counts or models structure globally; end each post with a compact checklist and a transition explaining what the next toolkit adds.\n\n11. **BUDGET:** Keep the final answer within 950 words: series introduction 35 words, three posts at about 290 words each (870 total), and closing progression/practice summary 45 words; within each post reserve roughly 80 words for concepts, 85 words per worked problem, and 40 words for heuristics and takeaways.\n\n12. **SAFETY:** Do not guess contest metadata or reproduce uncertain statements as exact quotations; flag any unavailable primary-source verification and paraphrase only when necessary while preserving the mathematical conditions.\n\nBLIND SCOUT INVENTORY\n\n[{\"kind\": \"scope\", \"requirement\": \"Topic map must include double counting, extremal principle, probabilistic method, plus a graph-modeling branch.\", \"must_mention\": [\"double counting\", \"extremal principle\", \"probabilistic method\"], \"reason\": \"A serious olympiad-combinatorics series should span counting, structural, existence, and modeling techniques rather than present disconnected elementary tricks.\", \"search_leads\": [\"standard olympiad combinatorics syllabus topics\"]}, {\"kind\": \"format\", \"requirement\": \"State an explicit post sequence whose later installments reuse techniques established in earlier installments.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"A series needs pedagogical progression and prerequisites, not merely an unordered topic inventory.\", \"search_leads\": [\"combinatorics olympiad course progression syllabus\"]}, {\"kind\": \"format\", \"requirement\": \"Apply the intuition→formal detail→sourced problem→complete solution→takeaway sequence in every post.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"The request repeats these deliverables implicitly for the series; providing them only selectively would be incomplete.\", \"search_leads\": [\"mathematics expository blog worked-example structure\"]}, {\"kind\": \"evidence\", \"requirement\": \"Include national and international examples, each labeled with exact contest, year, and problem number or shortlist code.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"Precise metadata permits verification, prevents invented attributions, and demonstrates the requested breadth of top-olympiad sources.\", 