 serious high-school olympiad students who know basic algebra, sets, induction, and elementary graphs but lack advanced combinatorics, defining all specialized notation and named methods before use.\n\n3. CORE: Sequence topics as forced structure through pigeonhole and extremal principle; structure under change through invariants, monovariants, and coloring; then global structure through double counting, bijections, and graph modeling, while briefly locating recursion, inclusion–exclusion, generating functions, and probabilistic method.\n\n4. CORE: In every post include a hook, prerequisites, intuitive explanation, precise definitions, variants, recognition cues, then two sourced problems—preferably accessible and harder—each presented through exploration, decisive insight, rigorous step-by-step solution, and takeaway.\n\n5. EVIDENCE: Select at least six national and international examples from multiple named sources such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, and the IMO Shortlist, labeling each with exact contest, year, problem number, or shortlist code verified against official archives.\n\n6. SAFETY: Never guess metadata or quote uncertain wording; flag unavailable primary verification, paraphrase only when necessary without changing mathematical conditions, and use reputable secondary repositories only for discovery or clearly identified backup.\n\n7. SAFETY: Independently verify every proof, justify each nontrivial implication, and check boundary conditions, equality or extremal cases, termination arguments, and construction validity where applicable, comparing against official or recognized solutions.\n\n8. SAFETY: For every major technique identify a common misuse, including poor pigeonhole partitions, unproved invariant preservation, nonterminating monovariants, mismatched counted objects, missed equality cases, or graph language that fails to simplify the problem.\n\n9. LATENT: Solve one featured problem by two genuinely different methods and compare their recogni